// trace.config — trace-file field set, applied to EVERY backend.
//
// Layered on top of the backend fragment with a second `-c`, so all five
// backends emit an identical trace schema. Keep it that way: the analysis
// scripts key on these column names, and the paper-data collector rejects a
// run whose required trace columns are absent.
//
// Why this file exists at all: Nextflow's DEFAULT field set is
//   task_id, hash, native_id, name, status, exit, submit, duration, realtime,
//   %cpu, peak_rss, peak_vmem, rchar, wchar
// which omits `start` and `complete`. Those two are required for the earliest
// task-attempt start reported per run, and for the peak ready-task count the
// paper reports as a workload descriptor.
//
// `raw = true` emits durations as integer milliseconds and memory as bytes,
// instead of the human-readable "2h 30m 15s" / "3.2 GB" renderings. The
// collectors do arithmetic on these columns, so raw values avoid a parsing
// step and the rounding that the pretty format bakes in.
//
// The CLI `-with-trace <file>` in examples/sbatch/*.sbatch sets trace.enabled
// and trace.file.
// It does NOT set fields or raw, which is why they live here.

trace {
    raw    = true
    fields = 'task_id,hash,native_id,process,name,status,exit,submit,start,complete,duration,realtime,%cpu,rss,peak_rss,vmem,peak_vmem,rchar,wchar,attempt'
}
